{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 44, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall come and look, and behold, it has spread.</b> One might think the returning affliction does not render unclean unless it spread — therefore it says 'a plague of leprosy in the walls' (צָרַעַת מַמְאֶרֶת בַּבָּתִּים), and it says 'a plague of leprosy in garments' (צָרַעַת מַמְאֶרֶת בַּבְּגָדִים). Just as there, it defiles the returning affliction even though it did not spread, so too here, it defiles the returning affliction even though it did not spread. If so, what is the meaning of 'and behold, it has spread'? This verse has no place here — rather, 'and he shall tear down the house' should have been written after 'and if the plague returns'; 'and he shall look, and behold, it has spread' comes only to teach about a plague that remained unchanged in its appearance during the first week, and at the end of the second week he comes and finds that it has spread — since Scripture did not specify anything above about one that remained unchanged in the first week — and it teaches you here, in this spreading, that it speaks only of one that remained unchanged in the first and spread in the second. And what does he do for it? One might think he tears it down, since 'and he shall tear down the house' is juxtaposed to it — therefore it says 'and the priest shall return' (וְשָׁב הַכֹּהֵן), and 'and the priest shall come' (וּבָא הַכֹּהֵן): we learn 'coming' from 'returning.' Just as 'returning' — he removes, scrapes, replasters, and gives it a week, so too 'coming' — he removes, scrapes, replasters, and gives it a week; and if it returns, he tears down; if it did not return, it is clean. And from where do we know that if it remained unchanged in both, he removes, scrapes, replasters, and gives it a week? Scripture says 'and if it should come, it shall come' (וְאִם בֹּא יָבֹא). What is Scripture speaking of? If of one that spread in the first — it has already been stated. If of one that spread in the second — it has already been stated. Rather, 'and if it should come, it shall come' says only about one that came at the end of the first week and came at the end of the second week and looked, and behold, it had not spread. This one that remained unchanged — what does he do for it? One might think he declares it clean and goes, as it is written here 'and he shall purify the house'? Therefore it says 'when the plague is healed' (כִּי נִרְפָּא הַנֶּגַע) — I declared clean only the healed one. What does he do for it? 'Coming' is stated above and 'coming' is stated below: just as in the upper one he removes, scrapes, replasters, and gives it a week — derived by gezerah shavah: this 'returning' is this 'coming' — so too in the lower one, and so forth, as is in Torat Kohanim. The conclusion of the matter: there is no tearing down except for a returning affliction after removal, scraping, and replastering, and the returning affliction does not require spreading. And the order of the verses is thus: 'and if it returns,' 'and he shall tear down,' 'and he who comes into the house,' 'and he who eats in the house,' 'and the priest shall come and look, and behold, it has spread' — and Scripture speaks of one that remained unchanged in the first, for which he gives a second week of quarantine; and at the end of the second week of quarantine he comes and sees that it has spread; and what does he do for it? He removes, scrapes, replasters, and gives it a week; it returned — he tears down; it did not return — it requires birds; for there are no more than three weeks in afflictions (see Sifra)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:39.659412727Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}